    "body": "Great on any Friday: Bluetooth-FM Transmitter for Car - AUX input + 2x Charging ports (USB-C & USB-A)\nJapan's 2027 Acura Integra Type S Coming December 2026 With a Six-Page List of Caveats\n36 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nThe first-ever Acura for Japan is here, with Honda confirming a December 18 launch date in the automaker's home market. Produced in the United States of America, the Integra Type S will ship to the Land of the Rising Sun with the steering wheel on the wrong side for left-hand traffic, an infotainment unit that speaks only English, and climate controls that refuse to use Celsius.\nThat's not all, though. Per a six-page document listing all caveats, the traffic-sign recognition system of the Integra Type S cannot accurately identify Japanese signs. Just like Toyota, Nissan, and Subaru, the folks at Honda are candid about the compromises associated with a left-hand-drive American vehicle in Japan. The document reads like a checklist of things that will not work as expected.\nJapanese customers in the market for the ITS will have to wait until December 18, and Honda is limiting the initial allocation to 250 vehicles. 30 of them will be sold through a lottery system, while 220 others will be handled by dealers. All hail from the Marysville plant in Ohio, and the suggested retail price of the Japanese-market Integra Type S is an eye-watering 8,998,000 yen (including consumption tax).\nThat's close to 56,500 dollars at current exchange rates, putting the Japanese ITS very close to its American counterpart. Slightly updated for model year 2027, the compact-sized liftback carries an MSRP of 54,600 bucks, plus 1,395 dollars for the destination charge. As ever, it flaunts a 2.0-liter turbo inline-four with 320 ponies to its name, a six-speed manual tranny, and a limited-slip differential.\nHonda is bringing the Integra Type S to Japan under a certification framework that makes it straightforward to sell American-built vehicles without going through the domestic approval process. The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport, and Tourism introduced it back in February 2026, and this framework essentially deems American vehicles compliant with all relevant Japanese standards.\nPhoto: Honda Beyond the Integra Type S, other confirmed models for the Japanese market include the Passport TrailSport Elite, Highlander, Tundra, Camry, Ascent, and Murano. Available exclusively for government and certain fleet buyers through the RMA Group, a bunch of Ford trucks are coming to Japan as well.\nAlthough the aforementioned framework removes the need for additional Japanese testing under the normal route, American-built vehicles still need ministerial approval. The American-market headlights of the Integra Type R have had their beam alignment altered for Japanese roads, and the license plate mounting setup is different as well.\nHonda further changed the Wi-Fi frequency to comply with domestic radio regulations. Localization efforts, however, leave much to be desired. The driver sits on the left instead of the right, and both the indicator and lighting stalk remain on the left side of the steering column.\nInfotainment menus are entirely in English, built-in navigation isn't available, SiriusXM cannot be used, and neither can Acura Link or System Update. Even the clock needs manual adjustment because the built-in software lacks a Japanese time zone setting. Indeed, plenty of compromises here.\nThe certification system used to bring the ITS to Japan will also be employed for the Passport TrailSport Elite, which is likely coming in the first quarter of 2027. Rather than Ohio, all versions of the US-built Passport are assembled at the Honda Alabama Auto Plant in Lincoln.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Acura Integra Type S Acura Integra Type S 2027 Acura Integra acura integra Acura Japan\nDownload: 2027 Acura Integra Type S for Japan instruction document (PDF)\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 of Lincoln's Most Unconventional Production Models",
